questions.explanation:
This technical challenge evaluates expertise in multi-data center deployment, dynamic certificate lifecycle management, mutual TLS implementation, and tenant-aware routing patterns. Key assessment areas include: Vault integration for secure secrets management, Docker secrets and configs utilization, Nginx TLS configuration with mTLS enforcement, header-based tenant identification and routing, blue-green canary deployment strategies across data centers, automated bootstrap scripting, and comprehensive observability integration.

```nginx
server {
  listen 443 ssl;
  ssl_certificate /secrets/tenant.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /secrets/tenant.key;
  ssl_client_certificate /secrets/ca.crt;
  ssl_verify_client on;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests secure, rotating certs in Swarm with Vault, handling cert storage (secret vs volume), and canary semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Swarm multi-node with TLS and mTLS
- Vault PKI for dynamic certs
- Certs via writable volume + sidecar refresh
- Canary rollout with health checks and rollback
- Nginx TLS termination and service-to-service mTLS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple cert refresh watcher from Vault to a mounted volume
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const fs = require('fs');
async function refresh() {
  const res = await fetch('https://vault.example/v1/pki/issue/api', { headers: { 'X-Vault-Token': process.env.VAULT_TOKEN } });
  const cert = await res.text();
  fs.writeFileSync('/certs/api.crt', cert);
}
setInterval(refresh, 5 * 60 * 1000);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cert rotation does not break existing connections?
- How would you monitor Vault token renewal and automatic revocation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic, per-tenant tracing in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, including config retrieval, TTL-based caching, and graceful degradation when the sidecar cannot fetch config.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry sampling and per-tenant configuration
- Consul KV as a central per-tenant policy store
- TTL caching to limit external calls
- Graceful fallback to sensible default rate
- Cross-DC deployment considerations and observability impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-sampler for per-tenant tracing
class TenantSampler {
  constructor(fetchRate) {
    this.fetchRate = fetchRate; // function(tenant) -> rate
    this.cache = new Map(); // tenant -> {rate, expiry}
  }
  getRate(tenant) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(tenant);
    if (entry && entry.expiry > Date.now()) return entry.rate;
    // fetch from Consul KV here, then cache for 5s
    const rate = this.fetchRate(tenant) ?? 20;
    this.cache.set(tenant, { rate, expiry: Date.now() + 5000 });
    return rate;
  }
  shouldSample(tenant) {
    const rate = this.getRate(tenant);
    return Math.random() * 100 < rate;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness of per-tenant rates during rollout?
- What are failure modes if Consul KV is unavailable, and how to mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic cross-DC routing control and zero-downtime config updates in docker-dca.

## Key Concepts
- etcd as central config store
- sidecar watching and config generation
- nginx config fragmenting and graceful reload
- cross-DC raft replication
- observability via Prometheus

